{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-556458",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt[3]{30}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect cubes that are just below and just above $30.$\n\nThe perfect cubes closest to $30$ are $27$ and $64.$ We have\n\n$$\n27<30<64.\n$$\n\nTaking the cube root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{27} & <\\sqrt[3]{30}<\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ 3 & <\\sqrt[3]{30}<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14185481/assets/3efc92b2664e8fade27edc67f977d78b317e73343d3201ac38c1fad807cdb01a.png)\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt[3]{30}$ lies between the consecutive whole numbers $3$ and $4.$ \n\nTherefore, since $a<b,$ we have $a=3$ and $b=4.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
